1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,236
A correct schema still leaks if every login can SELECT star from customers.

2
00:00:05,236 --> 00:00:12,541
Access control is who may read, write, change shape, or grant those powers to someone else.

3
00:00:12,541 --> 00:00:16,263
SQLite files are usually one user on disk.

4
00:00:16,263 --> 00:00:19,277
The operating system password is the lock.

5
00:00:19,277 --> 00:00:23,763
MySQL and PostgreSQL have users inside the server.

6
00:00:23,763 --> 00:00:26,736
GRANT SELECT ON orders TO analyst.

7
00:00:26,736 --> 00:00:29,875
REVOKE UPDATE ON orders FROM analyst.

8
00:00:29,875 --> 00:00:34,458
Least privilege means start with nothing, then add the verbs required.

9
00:00:34,458 --> 00:00:38,097
An analyst who never updates should not hold UPDATE.

10
00:00:38,097 --> 00:00:42,597
A checkout service needs INSERT on orders and SELECT on books.

11
00:00:42,597 --> 00:00:44,472
It does not need DROP TABLE.

12
00:00:44,472 --> 00:00:46,180
Roles group privileges.

13
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:49,527
Grant to a role, then grant the role to people.

14
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:54,805
When someone changes jobs, you revoke the role, not twenty tables by hand.

15
00:00:54,805 --> 00:00:56,763
Views can narrow columns.

16
00:00:56,763 --> 00:01:01,888
A view without email lets support read names without seeing mailboxes.

17
00:01:01,888 --> 00:01:05,180
GRANT SELECT on the view, not on the base table.

18
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:09,541
That is hide-columns as a security tool, not only a convenience.

19
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:13,263
Do not put the database root password in an application.

20
00:01:13,263 --> 00:01:17,708
The application gets a user that can do its job and nothing louder.

21
00:01:17,708 --> 00:01:22,541
Migrations may use a stricter user in a pipeline, not at runtime.

22
00:01:22,541 --> 00:01:25,972
Row-level policies exist in PostgreSQL.

23
00:01:25,972 --> 00:01:28,736
They filter which rows a user sees.

24
00:01:28,736 --> 00:01:31,000
That is a later, sharper tool.

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,208
Start with GRANT and views.

26
00:01:33,208 --> 00:01:34,652
Audit who has what.

27
00:01:34,652 --> 00:01:38,416
A privilege you cannot name is a privilege you should revoke.

28
00:01:38,416 --> 00:01:41,347
SQL injection is not a GRANT problem.

29
00:01:41,347 --> 00:01:43,291
It is a query-building problem.

30
00:01:43,291 --> 00:01:47,263
Prepared statements keep user text out of the statement shape.

31
00:01:47,263 --> 00:01:52,416
You still GRANT only what the app user needs, so a stolen query does less.

32
00:01:52,416 --> 00:01:53,847
Defense is layered.

33
00:01:53,847 --> 00:01:58,097
Schema, privileges, views, prepared statements.

34
00:01:58,097 --> 00:02:00,583
Write the job as verbs on tables.

35
00:02:00,583 --> 00:02:02,513
Then GRANT those verbs.

36
00:02:02,513 --> 00:02:03,527
Then stop.

37
00:02:03,527 --> 00:02:05,666
PUBLIC is a role you should look at.

38
00:02:05,666 --> 00:02:09,180
Some engines grant surprising defaults to PUBLIC.

39
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:11,902
REVOKE them when you mean least privilege.

40
00:02:11,902 --> 00:02:14,527
Table owners bypass some checks.

41
00:02:14,527 --> 00:02:18,847
Do not own application tables as the app user if you can avoid it.

42
00:02:18,847 --> 00:02:21,555
Own as a migrator, grant to the app.

43
00:02:21,555 --> 00:02:24,944
Column-level GRANT exists in PostgreSQL.

44
00:02:24,944 --> 00:02:29,694
GRANT SELECT (name, city) ON customers TO support.

45
00:02:29,694 --> 00:02:32,680
Views are still easier to explain to a human.

46
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:35,805
Use column grants when a view is too heavy.

47
00:02:35,805 --> 00:02:40,333
TEMPORARY tables and TEMP are session-scoped in many engines.

48
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:42,708
They do not replace privilege design.

49
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:47,180
A user who can create a temp table still should not SELECT emails.

50
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:52,638
Passwords in the server should be hashed, and rotation should be a calendar event.

51
00:02:52,638 --> 00:02:57,041
That is operations, and it still belongs next to GRANT in your head.

52
00:02:57,041 --> 00:03:01,208
A leaked read-only user is better than a leaked superuser.

53
00:03:01,208 --> 00:03:03,069
Design for leak size.

54
00:03:03,069 --> 00:03:06,472
Logs of GRANT changes are part of access control.

55
00:03:06,472 --> 00:03:10,583
If you cannot say who gave UPDATE last month, you cannot audit.

56
00:03:10,583 --> 00:03:11,902
Turn the logs on.

57
00:03:11,902 --> 00:03:13,500
The verbs were the start.

58
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:16,097
The job description is the GRANT list.

59
00:03:16,097 --> 00:03:17,319
Keep them equal.

60
00:03:17,319 --> 00:03:19,222
When they drift, revoke.

61
00:03:19,222 --> 00:03:22,736
Then the schema's privacy matches the schema's shape.

62
00:03:22,736 --> 00:03:27,055
If everyone is a superuser, you do not have access control.

63
00:03:27,055 --> 00:03:29,500
You have a shared login and a hope.

64
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:30,944
Hope is not a GRANT.

65
00:03:30,944 --> 00:03:34,486
Split migrator, app, analyst, support.

66
00:03:34,486 --> 00:03:36,361
Give each a verb list.

67
00:03:36,361 --> 00:03:37,763
Views for support.

68
00:03:37,763 --> 00:03:39,472
SELECT for analysts.

69
00:03:39,472 --> 00:03:41,333
INSERT where the app sells.

70
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:42,888
No DROP at runtime.

71
00:03:42,888 --> 00:03:44,750
Revoke PUBLIC extras.

72
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:46,125
Log the GRANTs.

73
00:03:46,125 --> 00:03:47,708
Prepare the statements.

74
00:03:47,708 --> 00:03:51,541
Layer those, and a stolen query is a smaller fire.

75
00:03:51,541 --> 00:03:53,541
Smaller fires are the design.

76
00:03:53,541 --> 00:03:55,513
Least privilege is the name.

77
00:03:55,513 --> 00:03:58,277
The job description is the implementation.

78
00:03:58,277 --> 00:04:02,416
Databases hold credentials, payments, and secrets.

79
00:04:02,416 --> 00:04:09,347
Two pillars protect them: access control for who may act, and cryptography for what a thief would see.

80
00:04:09,347 --> 00:04:13,930
Miss either, and a copied file or a curious intern is enough.

81
00:04:13,930 --> 00:04:18,847
Access control decides who can read, write, or administer.

82
00:04:18,847 --> 00:04:24,277
Discretionary access control is an administrator granting privileges by hand.

83
00:04:24,277 --> 00:04:25,611
It works on a toy.

84
00:04:25,611 --> 00:04:29,222
It does not scale past a handful of people who never leave.

85
00:04:29,222 --> 00:04:34,194
Mandatory access control assigns clearance levels to data and to people.

86
00:04:34,194 --> 00:04:39,083
A user may read a row only if their clearance meets the label on that data.

87
00:04:39,083 --> 00:04:45,166
Public, sensitive, confidential is a typical ladder in a school or hospital sketch.

88
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:48,934
Student names might be public inside the institution.

89
00:04:48,934 --> 00:04:50,670
Grades might be sensitive.

90
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:52,990
Identity documents confidential.

91
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:57,809
The engine, or the surrounding system, must actually enforce the labels.

92
00:04:57,809 --> 00:05:01,878
A comment in a wiki is not mandatory access control.

93
00:05:01,878 --> 00:05:08,253
Role-based access control groups privileges into roles: analyst, clerk, admin.

94
00:05:08,253 --> 00:05:13,045
You grant the role, not a thousand table privileges, to each person.

95
00:05:13,045 --> 00:05:16,267
When someone changes jobs, you revoke the role.

96
00:05:16,267 --> 00:05:20,003
When a job's duties change, you edit the role once.

97
00:05:20,003 --> 00:05:21,698
That is why roles scale.

98
00:05:21,698 --> 00:05:26,448
In SQL, GRANT SELECT ON orders TO analyst is the shape.

99
00:05:26,448 --> 00:05:28,073
REVOKE takes it back.

100
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:34,573
GRANT with GRANT OPTION lets a user pass privileges on, which is a power you should hand out rarely.

101
00:05:34,573 --> 00:05:38,115
Least privilege means a reporting user cannot DELETE.

102
00:05:38,115 --> 00:05:42,198
An application role that takes orders should not ALTER TABLE.

103
00:05:42,198 --> 00:05:47,670
Separate the account the app uses from the account a human uses to administer.

104
00:05:47,670 --> 00:05:49,087
Hashing is one-way.

105
00:05:49,087 --> 00:05:52,490
You store a hash of a password, not the password.

106
00:05:52,490 --> 00:05:56,531
When the user types it again, you hash the attempt and compare.

107
00:05:56,531 --> 00:06:00,809
A stolen table of hashes should not give readable passwords.

108
00:06:00,809 --> 00:06:10,240
Salt, a per-row random value mixed into the hash, stops a single rainbow table from cracking every user who chose the same password.

109
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:16,531
Use a slow password hash designed for passwords, not a fast general hash meant for files.

110
00:06:16,531 --> 00:06:19,045
Encryption is two-way with a key.

111
00:06:19,045 --> 00:06:25,990
At rest, a disk or column can be ciphertext so a stolen file is not a stolen diary.

112
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:31,753
In transit, connections use encryption so a packet sniff is not a password dump.

113
00:06:31,753 --> 00:06:37,823
Keys must live somewhere that is not the same stolen disk, or you encrypted with a sticky note.

114
00:06:37,823 --> 00:06:39,240
Hash passwords.

115
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:44,906
Encrypt secrets you must later read, like a stored token the app must present.

116
00:06:44,906 --> 00:06:48,045
Do not encrypt passwords you only need to check.

117
00:06:48,045 --> 00:06:53,198
You would have to decrypt to compare, and then you are back to storing something reversible.

118
00:06:53,198 --> 00:06:59,087
Access control without hashing still leaks passwords if the table is SELECT-able.

119
00:06:59,087 --> 00:07:05,823
Hashing without access control still lets anyone run SELECT on the hashes and attack them at leisure.

120
00:07:05,823 --> 00:07:07,906
Together they are the two doors.

121
00:07:07,906 --> 00:07:10,240
This lesson stays on those doors.

122
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:12,392
It is not a cloud console tour.

123
00:07:12,392 --> 00:07:19,101
It is GRANT, roles, labels, hashes, salts, and encryption used on purpose.

124
00:07:19,101 --> 00:07:26,281
Write down who may read orders, who may update them, and whether a password is ever stored in the clear.

125
00:07:26,281 --> 00:07:29,837
If the answer to the last is yes, stop and hash.

126
00:07:29,837 --> 00:07:34,212
If everyone shares one admin login, stop and make roles.

127
00:07:34,212 --> 00:07:38,378
Then the valuable rows have a chance of staying boring to an attacker.

128
00:07:38,378 --> 00:07:42,448
Hashing is not encryption, said again with the school passwords.

129
00:07:42,448 --> 00:07:46,698
Counselors do not decrypt a password to help a student log in.

130
00:07:46,698 --> 00:07:49,573
They reset, which writes a new hash.

131
00:07:49,573 --> 00:07:53,990
Encryption would have let them read the old password, which you never want.

132
00:07:53,990 --> 00:08:02,476
Access control says counselors may UPDATE password_hash and not SELECT other students' grades if that is the policy.

133
00:08:02,476 --> 00:08:09,823
GRANT UPDATE on that column, or on a procedure that resets, is tighter than GRANT UPDATE on the whole table.

134
00:08:09,823 --> 00:08:14,226
Procedures and column grants are how least privilege gets specific.

135
00:08:14,226 --> 00:08:18,601
If the engine lacks column grants, split confidential columns.

136
00:08:18,601 --> 00:08:25,198
Splitting for security is still normalization-shaped: a table whose only job is secrets.

137
00:08:25,198 --> 00:08:27,642
Point at it with a key from students.

138
00:08:27,642 --> 00:08:30,809
GRANT on the secrets table to counselors only.

139
00:08:30,809 --> 00:08:35,295
Now a leaked SELECT star on students is not a leaked id number.

140
00:08:35,295 --> 00:08:40,003
That design is the long security film, in fundamentals language.

141
00:08:40,003 --> 00:08:43,642
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

142
00:08:43,642 --> 00:08:46,698
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

143
00:08:46,698 --> 00:08:47,906
Two pillars?

144
00:08:47,906 --> 00:08:52,962
Access control for who may act, cryptography for what a thief would see.

145
00:08:52,962 --> 00:08:54,753
Discretionary access?

146
00:08:54,753 --> 00:08:56,448
Hand-granted privileges.

147
00:08:56,448 --> 00:08:57,698
Fine for a toy.

148
00:08:57,698 --> 00:08:58,976
Does not scale.

149
00:08:58,976 --> 00:09:00,642
Mandatory access?

150
00:09:00,642 --> 00:09:03,476
Clearance labels on data and on people.

151
00:09:03,476 --> 00:09:05,087
Role-based access?

152
00:09:05,087 --> 00:09:08,684
Privileges bundled in roles you grant and revoke.

153
00:09:08,684 --> 00:09:09,740
GRANT shape?

154
00:09:09,740 --> 00:09:12,712
GRANT SELECT ON orders TO analyst.

155
00:09:12,712 --> 00:09:13,642
REVOKE?

156
00:09:13,642 --> 00:09:17,448
Takes the privilege or role back the day someone leaves.

157
00:09:17,448 --> 00:09:18,503
GRANT OPTION?

158
00:09:18,503 --> 00:09:20,837
Lets a user pass privileges on.

159
00:09:20,837 --> 00:09:21,615
Rare.

160
00:09:21,615 --> 00:09:22,878
Least privilege?

161
00:09:22,878 --> 00:09:25,031
The app cannot ALTER TABLE.

162
00:09:25,031 --> 00:09:27,003
A reporter cannot DELETE.

163
00:09:27,122 --> 00:09:29,335
Why split confidential columns?

164
00:09:29,335 --> 00:09:33,099
If you cannot GRANT by column, make a secrets table.

165
00:09:33,099 --> 00:09:34,752
Hash versus encrypt?

166
00:09:34,752 --> 00:09:36,877
Hash is one-way for passwords.

167
00:09:36,877 --> 00:09:40,280
Encrypt is two-way for secrets you must read back.

168
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:41,113
Salt?

169
00:09:41,113 --> 00:09:46,766
Per-row randomness so one rainbow table cannot crack every identical password.

170
00:09:46,766 --> 00:09:48,710
Why not encrypt passwords?

171
00:09:48,710 --> 00:09:52,863
You would decrypt to compare, which means reversible storage.

172
00:09:52,863 --> 00:09:55,224
Why not hash tokens you must send later?

173
00:09:55,224 --> 00:09:57,933
You cannot recover the token from a hash.

174
00:09:57,933 --> 00:09:59,335
Plaintext allowed?

175
00:09:59,335 --> 00:10:03,113
For public facts like a course title, not for secrets.

176
00:10:03,113 --> 00:10:05,599
GRANT SELECT to everyone on hashes?

177
00:10:05,599 --> 00:10:07,627
Still a gift to attackers.

178
00:10:07,627 --> 00:10:10,210
Locked GRANT with plaintext passwords?

179
00:10:10,210 --> 00:10:12,335
A gift to a backup thief.

180
00:10:12,335 --> 00:10:14,113
Column comment worth writing?

181
00:10:14,113 --> 00:10:17,474
Hashed, encrypted, or plain, and why.

182
00:10:17,474 --> 00:10:18,960
Clerk versus admin?

183
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:20,155
Different roles.

184
00:10:20,155 --> 00:10:22,558
Never one shared admin login.

185
00:10:22,558 --> 00:10:24,419
When do you review privileges?

186
00:10:24,419 --> 00:10:28,752
Hire, leave, and when a table gains a sensitive column.

187
00:10:28,752 --> 00:10:30,169
Is this a cloud tour?

188
00:10:30,169 --> 00:10:30,933
No.

189
00:10:30,933 --> 00:10:36,197
GRANT, roles, labels, hashes, salts, encryption.

190
00:10:36,197 --> 00:10:37,530
Reset password?

191
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:38,835
Write a new hash.

192
00:10:38,835 --> 00:10:40,627
Do not decrypt an old one.

193
00:10:40,627 --> 00:10:42,016
Backup files?

194
00:10:42,016 --> 00:10:46,447
Encrypt at rest so a stolen file is not a stolen diary.

195
00:10:46,447 --> 00:10:47,572
Connections?

196
00:10:47,572 --> 00:10:49,141
Encrypt in transit.

197
00:10:49,141 --> 00:10:50,502
Where do keys live?

198
00:10:50,502 --> 00:10:53,766
Not on the same stolen disk as the ciphertext.

199
00:10:53,766 --> 00:10:58,030
Public students, sensitive grades, confidential ids?

200
00:10:58,030 --> 00:11:00,877
A clearance ladder you must actually enforce.

201
00:11:00,877 --> 00:11:02,516
Views that hide names?

202
00:11:02,516 --> 00:11:04,752
A way to GRANT analysts less.

203
00:11:04,752 --> 00:11:06,905
App account versus human admin?

204
00:11:06,905 --> 00:11:07,863
Separate.

205
00:11:07,863 --> 00:11:10,933
If a line in the security design is TBD?

206
00:11:10,933 --> 00:11:12,891
Do not load real people yet.

207
00:11:12,891 --> 00:11:14,474
Why is the film long?

208
00:11:14,474 --> 00:11:17,974
Two doors, walked, with people in the schema.

209
00:11:17,974 --> 00:11:19,377
What do you refuse?

210
00:11:19,377 --> 00:11:23,794
TEXT passwords and a login named admin shared by all.

211
00:11:23,794 --> 00:11:28,516
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

212
00:11:28,516 --> 00:11:32,530
Walk GRANT from a blank engine user who can only connect.

213
00:11:32,530 --> 00:11:39,197
CREATE ROLE clerk, CREATE ROLE analyst, CREATE ROLE app, CREATE ROLE registrar.

214
00:11:39,197 --> 00:11:42,835
GRANT SELECT INSERT UPDATE ON students TO clerk.

215
00:11:42,835 --> 00:11:45,516
GRANT SELECT ON courses TO clerk.

216
00:11:45,516 --> 00:11:48,599
GRANT SELECT ON enrollments TO analyst.

217
00:11:48,599 --> 00:11:51,766
GRANT INSERT UPDATE ON enrollments TO app.

218
00:11:51,766 --> 00:11:56,655
GRANT ALL ON ALL TABLES to registrar is tempting and usually too wide.

219
00:11:56,655 --> 00:12:01,766
Registrar needs grades and ids; analyst does not need ids.

220
00:12:01,766 --> 00:12:05,919
If ALL TABLES is how ids leak, list tables instead.

221
00:12:05,919 --> 00:12:10,863
REVOKE INSERT ON students FROM clerk when clerks should not invent people.

222
00:12:10,863 --> 00:12:18,363
A procedure reset_password that only updates password_hash is tighter than column guesswork.

223
00:12:18,363 --> 00:12:23,960
GRANT EXECUTE on that procedure to helpdesk, not UPDATE on the whole users table.

224
00:12:23,960 --> 00:12:30,849
Mandatory labels on rows, when you have them, mean a volunteer cannot UNION their way around a role.

225
00:12:30,849 --> 00:12:39,072
Without row labels, two tables students_public and students_confidential are the poor person's MAC.

226
00:12:39,072 --> 00:12:43,058
Do not put the confidential table in the same PUBLIC synonym.

227
00:12:43,058 --> 00:12:49,863
Hashing parameters belong in a documented standard: algorithm, iterations, salt length.

228
00:12:49,863 --> 00:12:52,419
Do not invent a fast homemade hash.

229
00:12:52,419 --> 00:13:02,266
Encryption algorithms likewise: use what your platform already supports for columns or files, and keep keys in a vault the app reads at start.

230
00:13:02,266 --> 00:13:10,016
Rotating keys means decrypting with the old and encrypting with the new, which is why reversible secrets are expensive.

231
00:13:10,016 --> 00:13:15,335
Passwords never rotate by decryption; they rotate by user reset.

232
00:13:15,335 --> 00:13:25,266
Audit who has GRANT OPTION once a quarter even in a fundamentals class project, because copies of class databases become real faster than you think.

233
00:13:25,266 --> 00:13:29,738
A shared homework login is the admin-named-admin problem in costume.

234
00:13:29,738 --> 00:13:32,530
Give each human a user, then a role.

235
00:13:32,530 --> 00:13:35,738
Give the application a user, then a tighter role.

236
00:13:35,738 --> 00:13:37,294
Never reuse those.

237
00:13:37,294 --> 00:13:44,155
SELECT current privilege list after every GRANT until reading grants is a habit like reading WHERE.

238
00:13:44,155 --> 00:13:47,891
Security that you cannot list is security you imagined.

239
00:13:47,891 --> 00:13:48,724
List it.

240
00:13:48,724 --> 00:13:49,794
Then hashing.

241
00:13:49,794 --> 00:13:51,085
Then backups.

242
00:13:51,085 --> 00:13:57,072
Then you are in the same ballpark as a twenty-minute security lecture instead of a three-minute slogan.

243
00:13:57,072 --> 00:13:59,002
The slogan was two doors.

244
00:13:59,002 --> 00:14:08,335
The lecture is walking through them with GRANT, roles, labels, hashes, salts, and encryption until the design has no TBD.

245
00:14:08,335 --> 00:14:09,919
Close the TBD.

